WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:06.460
Armed with the transverse convolution, you're now ready to dive into the details of the

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.400
UNET architecture.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:14.300
In this video, let's first go over the architecture quickly to build intuition about how the UNET

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:19.520
works, and in the next video, we'll go through the details together.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.540
Let's dig in.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:28.680
Here's a rough diagram of the neural network architecture for semantic segmentation.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:35.960
We use normal convolutions for the first part of the neural network, and similar to the earlier

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:41.760
neural networks that you've seen, this part of the neural network will compress the image.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.440
You've gone from a very large image to one where the height and width of this activation

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.320
is much smaller.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.400
You've lost a lot of spatial information because the dimension is much smaller, but it's much

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.900
deeper.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:01.460
For example, this middle layer may represent that looks like there's a cat roughly in the

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:07.340
lower right hand portion of the image, but the detailed spatial information is lost because

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.820
the height and width is much smaller.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:15.180
Then the second half of this neural network uses the transverse convolution to blow the

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:20.420
representation size up back to the size of the original input image.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:25.020
Now it turns out that there's one modification to this architecture that would make it work

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.020
much better.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:32.460
That's what would turn this into the unit architecture, which is that skip connections

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:41.120
from the earlier layers to the later layers like this so that this earlier block of activations

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.500
is copied directly to this later block.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.100
Why do we want to do this?

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:54.460
Turns out that for this next to final layer to decide which region is the cat, two types

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.420
of information are useful.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:01.800
One is the high level spatial, high level contextual information, which it gets from

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:07.700
this previous layer, where hopefully the neural network will have figured out that in the

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:13.100
lower right hand corner of the image or maybe in the right part of the image, there's some

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.300
cat-like stuff going on.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:21.940
But what is missing is the very detailed fine grained spatial information because this

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.700
set of activations here has lower spatial resolution.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.560
The height and width is just lower.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.940
So what the skip connection does is it allows the neural network to take this very high

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:40.780
resolution low level feature information where it could capture for every pixel position

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:46.420
how much furry stuff is there in this pixel and use the skip connection to pause that

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.980
directly to this later layer.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:55.860
And so this way, this layer has both the lower resolution but high level spatial, high

00:02:55.860 --> 00:03:03.340
level contextual information, as well as the lower level but more detailed texture like

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:09.180
information in order to make a decision as to whether a certain pixel is part of a cat

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.340
or not.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:17.220
In this video, you saw just a brief high level intuition about how UNet works.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:20.660
Let's go on to the next video to see the details of how you can implement it.
